==English==
{{wikipedia}}
===Etymology===
1950s, {{etyl|fr}} {{term|auteur||[[author]]|lang=fr}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|la}} {{term|auctor|lang=la}}, from which also English {{term|author|lang=en}}.

In technical English sense of “artist expressing their personal vision”, coined in 1954 by [[w:François Roland Truffaut|François Truffaut]] in his French essay “Une certaine tendance du cinéma français” (“A certain tendency in French cinema”) in the influential film journal ''{{w|Cahiers du cinéma}},'' as the phrase “la politique des Auteurs”. Subsequently popularized in English in the 1960s; in the US by American film critic {{w|Andrew Sarris}}. See {{w|auteur theory}} for further discussion.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/o.tœːr/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An artist, often a film or theatre [[director]], whose complete control over all aspects of a production gives the end result a recognisable feel.

